From a6a0637377152cca63d4f83fee3d17fbea71d797 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:25:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(lifecycle-guard): mask data-consumer heredocs behind
 wrappers, in functions, nested; call-syntax paths are mentions

The terminal lifecycle guard refused read-only commands because a SQLite file named inside
interpreter code was read as an executed script (larger than the scan cap -> fail closed).
`strip_inert_heredoc_bodies` recognized a quoted data-consumer heredoc only as a bare top-level
`python3 - <<'PY'`.

tools/shell_heredoc.py
- Classify a quoted heredoc by its peeled consumer, reusing lifecycle_guard's
  `_peel_transparent_prefixes` (timeout N, nice, env VAR=..., command, ...). Only wrappers that
  hand stdin to their operand qualify; su/runuser/sudo -s/env -S stay visible.
- Accept positional args after the stdin marker (`python3 - "$1"`) and an opener that is the
  first command of a `name() {` / `function name {` body.
- A quoted outer body (`ssh h "bash -s" <<'R'`, also via `$SSH`) is literal text: data-consumer
  heredocs nested in it are masked by the same rules (depth-bounded); the outer body's own lines
  are still walked. Unquoted outer bodies are never recursed into.
- Mask nothing when the command defines a function or alias named like a consumer or wrapper
  (`python3() { bash; }`, `function python3 {`, `alias python3=bash`): main already refused the
  `;`-separated shadow; the newline-separated forms were masked on main.

cron/lifecycle_guard.py
- A segment that follows `(` after an ordinary word is interpreter call syntax
  (`expanduser("~/x")`, `connect('/d/x.db')`), not a subshell: its paths are mentions
  (read and scanned, never a fail-closed verdict). `$(`, `<(`, `>(`, `x=(`, and `(` after
  keywords (if/while/!/time/...) keep the executed treatment. This is script B's trigger.

Tests: 9 -> 63 cases in tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_heredoc_walk.py, plus verbatim
fixtures for both refused scripts (core_split_0923.sh sha256 4d87a461...).

Kanban: t_01a1d0ea6db6c158
